<InfoPanel Title="What OperationsFlow proves"
           Subtitle="The project now covers both modern Microsoft 365 workflows and traditional SQL-backed internal business app patterns."
           Tone="default">

    <div class="of-review-proof-grid">
        <div class="of-review-proof-card">
            <span class="of-badge of-badge-info">Microsoft 365 lane</span>
            <h3>Cloud business workflow direction</h3>
            <p>
                OperationsFlow is shaped for Microsoft OAuth2, SharePoint/Graph storage, Teams/Outlook follow-up, and Power BI reporting.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="of-review-proof-card">
            <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">SQL business app lane</span>
            <h3>Local/internal app direction</h3>
            <p>
                OperationsFlow includes SQL-backed local users, hashed passwords, roles, permissions, and account-linking preparation.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="of-review-proof-card">
            <span class="of-badge of-badge-warning">Evidence lane</span>
            <h3>Proof tied to work</h3>
            <p>
                Work Orders, Corrective Actions, and Document Intake can carry supporting files instead of relying on scattered folders or inboxes.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="of-review-proof-card">
            <span class="of-badge of-badge-muted">Management lane</span>
            <h3>Reviewable operations</h3>
            <p>
                Data Quality and Reports make missing evidence, overdue work, and review gaps visible to managers.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</InfoPanel>

<TableCard Title="Business problems addressed"
           Subtitle="OperationsFlow targets common problems in small-to-medium operational businesses.">

    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Problem</th>
                <th>OperationsFlow Response</th>
                <th>Business Value</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Work is spread across emails, spreadsheets, chats, and memory.</td>
                <td>Workflow records centralise Work Orders, Corrective Actions, Document Intake, Risk, Training, and review work.</td>
                <td>Creates a single review surface for operational follow-up.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Evidence is stored separately from the work it supports.</td>
                <td>RecordAttachments links files directly to records and exposes them in a central Document Library.</td>
                <td>Makes supporting proof easier to find during management, safety, admin, or audit review.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Managers cannot easily see incomplete or weak records.</td>
                <td>Data Quality flags missing evidence for important overdue/high/needs-review records.</td>
                <td>Turns review into action instead of manual checking.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Reports are often manual screenshots or spreadsheet exports.</td>
                <td>Reports summarise evidence coverage, missing evidence, overdue work, and management actions.</td>
                <td>Creates repeatable management reporting and a Power BI-ready direction.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Internal apps often need local users/roles, not just cloud-only auth.</td>
                <td>Week 3 implements SQL-backed local users, password login, roles, and permissions.</td>
                <td>Fits traditional internal business app environments and SQL Server/SQL Express workplaces.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Modern workplaces still need Microsoft 365 integration.</td>
                <td>Week 4 direction adds Microsoft OAuth2, account linking, SharePoint/Graph storage, and Microsoft 365 configuration.</td>
                <td>Shows a realistic path from local prototype to Microsoft 365 production implementation.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</TableCard>

<div class="of-two-column-grid">
    <InfoPanel Title="Value for Microsoft 365-focused employers"
               Subtitle="This lane fits organisations using SharePoint, Teams, Outlook, Power Automate, or Power BI."
               Tone="info">

        <ul class="of-mini-list">
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>SharePoint readiness:</strong>
                <span>Evidence workflow is already provider-shaped for a SharePoint file provider.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Graph direction:</strong>
                <span>Week 4 can implement upload/download/delete behaviour through Microsoft Graph.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>OAuth2:</strong>
                <span>Microsoft identity can link to existing LocalUser records.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Automation:</strong>
                <span>Reminders and follow-up workflow can later connect to Teams/Outlook/Power Automate.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Reporting:</strong>
                <span>CSV exports and structured review data support a Power BI path.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </InfoPanel>

    <InfoPanel Title="Value for SQL/internal app employers"
               Subtitle="This lane fits traditional business systems, SQL Server, and internal software environments."
               Tone="success">

        <ul class="of-mini-list">
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>SQL-backed accounts:</strong>
                <span>LocalUsers, LocalRoles, LocalUserRoles, LocalPermissions, and RolePermissions.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Password login:</strong>
                <span>Local username/password authentication with hashed passwords.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Persistent session:</strong>
                <span>Refresh-safe local login that reloads roles and permissions from SQL.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>Audit direction:</strong>
                <span>Activity Log records workflow and evidence actions.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                <strong>OAuth2 linking path:</strong>
                <span>ExternalLoginLinks supports Microsoft login mapped to local app accounts.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </InfoPanel>
</div>

